<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">11.4. Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Suppose $$\sum a_{n}$$ and $$\sum b_{n}$$ are series with positive terms and $$\sum b_{n}$$ is known to be convergent.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>If $$a_{n} &gt; b_{n}$$ for all $$n$$ , what can you say about $$\sum a_{n}$$ ? Why?</item>
          <item>If $$a_{n} &lt; b_{n}$$ for all $$n$$ , what can you say about $$\sum a_{n}$$ ? Why?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Nothing</paragraph>
        <paragraph>C</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Suppose $$\sum a_{n}$$ and $$\sum b_{n}$$ are series with positive terms and $$\sum b_{n}$$ is known to be divergent.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>If $$a_{n} &gt; b_{n}$$ for all $$n$$ , what can you say about $$\sum a_{n}$$ ? Why?</item>
          <item>If $$a_{n} &lt; b_{n}$$ for all $$n$$ , what can you say about $$\sum a_{n}$$ ? Why?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Use the Direct Comparison Test to show that the first series converges by comparing it to the second series. $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{n}{n^{3} + 5} \sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{2}}$$</item>
          <item>Use the Limit Comparison Test to show that that the first series converges by comparing it to the second series. $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{n}{n^{3} - 5} \sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{2}}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Use the Direct Comparison Test to show that the first series diverges by comparing it to the second series. $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{n^{2} + n}{n^{3} - 2} \sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}$$</item>
          <item>Use the Limit Comparison Test to show that that the first series diverges by comparing it to the second series. $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{n^{2} - n}{n^{3} + 2} \sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="5" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Which of the following inequalities can be used to show that $$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}n/(n^{3} + 1)$$ converges?</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\frac{n}{n^{3} + 1} \geq \frac{1}{n^{3} + 1}$$</item>
          <item>$$\frac{n}{n^{3} + 1} \leq \frac{1}{n}$$</item>
          <item>$$\frac{n}{n^{3} + 1} \leq \frac{1}{n^{2}}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>(c)</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
